## Reading and showing values

Everything the REPL prints is spent context, so it prints nothing on its own and
gives you commands to say what you want to see. Two moves cover almost
everything: head, tail and grep pull a value out, which the REPL names, and
echo shows a rendering of it.

### The REPL is silent: it names your result and describes its shape

Nothing is printed unless you echo it. Every result is bound to a variable —
named after the function that produced it — and reported as one line saying
what is in it:

(acme/list-issues :query "auth bug")
acme/list-issues-1: list of 27 alists, keys "id" "identifier" "title" "state" "url"

(+ 1 2)
+-1: 3

A small value is reported as itself; a large one is described: list of 27
alists with its keys, or 3412 words, or 200 characters. Either way **the variable
holds the whole value.** A setq or defun is reported under the name it
already bound, and nil and t report plainly — there is nothing to name.

**Never retype data the REPL produced.** Refer to the variable: it holds the
exact value, costs a handful of tokens, and cannot be misremembered. Re-emitting
a list, an id, a title or a body by hand is wasted output and the main way you
produce wrong data. Build on the name —
(mapcar (lambda (i) (cdr (assoc "id" i))) acme/list-issues-1).

To look at a result, name it in a form: (echo acme/list-issues-1). Writing the
bare name acme/list-issues-1 on a line of its own is a bare value, see "Syntax" —
it is prose, it evaluates nothing, and a reply that is nothing but prose ENDS THE
LOOP with that line as your final answer.
This is the most expensive typo in the language.

### Extract into a name, then echo a rendering of it

The shape line tells you what you need to write the next form. A bare head or
tail is the exception that needs neither step: it prints its slice on the spot.

To answer with something the REPL produced, compute the answer and echo THAT:

(grep page-1 "https?://[^ ]+")
grep-1: ("https://x.dev/a")

(echo (car grep-1))
https://x.dev/a

A bare slice is already the answer to "what is in there":

(head acme/list-issues-1 2)
((("id" . "a1f") ("identifier" . "ENG-12") ("title" . "Auth token refresh fails"))
 (("id" . "c3d") ("identifier" . "ENG-31") ("title" . "OAuth callback drops the state param")))

To render it for the user rather than read it yourself, keep the whole list and
echo the rendering:

(defun row (i) (concat "| " (cdr (assoc "identifier" i)) " | " (cdr (assoc "title" i)) " |"))
row: function

(echo (string-join (mapcar row (head acme/list-issues-1 4)) "\n"))
| ENG-12 | Auth token refresh fails |
| ENG-31 | OAuth callback drops the state param |

Both wrong versions look like this — never do either:

(echo "https://x.dev/a")                      the URL typed from a printout
(echo "| ENG-12 | Auth token refresh fails |")   the rows typed out by hand

You cannot retype what you were never shown, which is why the REPL describes a
result instead of printing it. Prefer computing over reading: (length x),
mapcar, assoc and grep work on the complete value, and one form that
extracts what you need beats paging through text.

### Echo output is capped for you, not for the user

A step may echo a fixed number of words. Past that the text you see stops and a
... line says how much is left and how to continue:

(echo report-1)
Q3 revenue grew 12 percent, driven by the two enterprise
... 10 of 40 words shown, 30 below — read on with (echo report-1 :offset 10)

The user reading the conversation sees the whole output; the cap is on your copy
alone. So when you see that line: do not re-run the call, and do not answer from
the fragment — page on with the offset it gave you, or use
(echo report-1 :match "pattern") when you know what you are looking for. Output
that is one unbroken word, minified JSON say, is cut by character instead, and
the ... line points at substring. Several echoes in one step share the budget;
when it runs out you are told how many words you were not shown.

### Windowing what echo prints

These options extend the core echo, see "Primitive built-ins".

- (echo x :offset 0 :length n) print a window of x, counted in
  whitespace-separated words; the ... line it ends with gives the offset to
  continue from.
- (echo x :match "re" :context 8 :max 10 :ignore-case t) print only the
  regions matching a regular expression, each as an @ and a word offset, plus
  the surrounding words with the match wrapped in double square brackets. Feed a
  reported offset back in as :offset. Use this to READ a value; use grep to KEEP
  what matched.
- (doc name) prints whole and costs nothing against the step's echo budget, and
  mints no name: the entry it printed is the report. Look freely.

### Extracting a value to keep it

These RETURN a value, so the REPL names it instead of printing. Extract into a
name, then echo a rendering of it.

- (head x [n]) the first n of x; (tail x [n]) the last n. On a list,
  n ELEMENTS — (head issues 4) is the first four rows. On text, n words.
  A slice is taken to be read, so one written as a step of its own is PRINTED
  and gets no name: (head issues 4) shows you the four rows, and there is no
  (echo …) to write after it. Nested in another form it prints nothing and is
  just the value — (mapcar row (head issues 4)), (setq top (head issues 4)).
  The discovery calls, see "MCP servers" — search-mcps, search-tools, list-tools,
  list-toolkit, list-mcps — print the same way for the same reason: you asked
  what is available in order to read it, so the answer is shown in full and
  there is no (echo …) step to follow it with.
- (grep x "pattern" :group n :max n :ignore-case t) what pattern matched, as
  a list, or nil. On a list, the ELEMENTS whose printed form matches:
  (grep issues "auth") is the issues about auth. On text, the matched
  substrings: (grep page "https?://[^ ]+") is the URLs in it.

### Gotchas

- The REPL prints NOTHING on its own: it reports a result's name and shape.
  The echo built-in is the only way to see a value, and the only way to show one
  to the user.
- Retyping data the REPL produced is the main way you produce wrong data.
  Extract it with grep or head and echo the variable instead.
- The head, tail and grep built-ins RETURN a value; only echo prints — except a
  bare head or tail, and a bare discovery call, which the REPL prints instead of
  describing, because both are asked for in order to be read.
- Echo output is capped for you but not for the user. Output ending in a
  ... line is not everything — page on with the offset it gives you.
- A hash-and-angle-brackets printout — of a promise, of a secret naming its key,
  of a closure — is a printout of a value that cannot be read back, so typing one
  is always an error. Use the name the value was reported under.
